-### FilterGS
-Proposes a traversal-free, parallel filtering structure that selects a single scale per Gaussian via a learned network, avoiding expensive tree traversal [S1, unofficial].
+A method focusing on accelerating the forward process by partitioning the screen into finer tiles and scheduling them on a multi-GPU pipeline [S1, unofficial].
 
 ### 4D Gaussian Splatting Motion Evaluation
-Renders time-varying captures by storing static attributes and per-splat motion coefficients in textures, evaluated in the vertex shader from a time uniform, avoiding per-frame CPU sorting [S6, unofficial].
+A technique for rendering time-varying 3D captures (volumetric video) by storing static per-splat attributes and per-splat motion coefficients in extra textures, evaluated in the vertex shader from a time uniform, avoiding per-frame CPU sorting and texture uploads [S6, unofficial]. It stores static per-splat attributes (position, scale, rotation, color) in standard splat buffers and per-splat motion coefficients (linear or polynomial trajectory parameters) and temporal opacity window parameters in extra textures [S6, unofficial]. The current playback time is passed as a uniform to the vertex shader, where motion coefficients are evaluated to compute current position and opacity [S6, unofficial]. Sorting is performed only occasionally, as motions are assumed to be small enough to not require per-frame sorting for visual correctness [S6, unofficial].
 
 ### AABB Culling for Gaussian Splatting
-Uses axis-aligned bounding boxes of projected ellipses (solved via partial derivatives) to cull tiles, addressing the inability of bounding circles to cull low-opacity pairs in the minor axis direction [S7].
+A culling technique that uses an axis-aligned bounding box of the projected Gaussian ellipse to reduce ineffective rendering expenses, specifically addressing the inability of bounding circles to cull low-opacity pairs in the minor axis direction [S7]. It sets the partial derivatives of the ellipse function $F = Ax^2 + By^2 + Cxy + D$ to 0 to find extrema, solving for $x_{max}$ and $y_{max}$ to construct the bounding box [S7]. Tiles are then culled based on this bounding box [S7].
 
 ### Load Balancing for Pixel-parallel Splatting
-Addresses uneven computational load across pixel threads by reducing Gaussians for heavy-load pixels and increasing for light-load pixels, using a loss weight $\lambda_{L1} + \lambda_{ssim} = 0.55$ [S7].
+A strategy to address uneven computational load across pixel threads in Gaussian rasterization by reducing the number of Gaussians rendered for heavy-load pixels and increasing it for light-load pixels [S7]. It uses a load balancing loss with weight $\lambda_{load}$ (typically 0.45) and reduces the sum of weights for remaining losses to 0.55 [S7].
 
 ### Tight Quad Rasterization with Eigenvalue Decomposition
-Represents Gaussians as non-axis-aligned quads using 2D eigenvalue decomposition, incorporating alpha into bound rectangle calculation for tighter fits and reduced overdraw [S8].
+Represents each 3D Gaussian as a quad in Gaussian rasterization [S8]. It decomposes eigenvalues of anisotropic 3D Gaussians into rotation and scale components (2D Eigenvalues in screenspace) [S8]. It computes a non-axis-aligned quad and incorporates splat alpha value into the bound rectangle calculation for tighter fits [S8].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot T^2)$ in the worst case where $N$ is the number of Gaussians and $T^2$ is the tile size, but effectively $O(K)$ where $K$ is the number of visible splat-pixel intersections due to early termination and culling [S5].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N + W \cdot H)$ for storing Gaussian attributes and output buffers. Shared memory usage per tile is minimal [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Enables real-time rendering (100+ FPS) and accelerated training on modern GPUs (e.g., RTX 3090/4090) for scenes with millions of Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** High throughput due to shared memory usage and coalesced memory access; differentiable for end-to-end training; simple implementation on CUDA [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Restricted to pinhole cameras; linear scaling with Gaussian density for small splats (wasted computation on empty pixels within large tiles); sensitive to tile size selection [S5].
-*   **When to Use:** Real-time rendering and training of static or slowly moving 3D Gaussian Splatting scenes on GPU.
-*   **When Not to Use:** Scenes requiring complex light transport (reflections, refractions), non-pinhole cameras, or extreme mobile constraints without specialized variants (e.g., Mobile-GS) [S5].
+**Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot T)$ where $N$ is the number of Gaussians and $T$ is the number of tiles, but effectively reduced by early termination and culling. The sorting step is $O(N \log N)$ or $O(N)$ with radix sort [S1, S3, unofficial].
+**Memory Complexity:** $O(N + W \cdot H)$ for storing Gaussian attributes, sorted indices, and tile bins [S1, S3, unofficial].
+**Performance:** Achieves real-time rendering speeds (e.g., >200 FPS) on modern GPUs by minimizing memory traffic through shared memory usage within tiles [S1, unofficial][S5].
+**Strengths:** High performance, differentiable (supports gradient computation for training), and scalable to large scenes [S1, S2, unofficial].
+**Limitations:** Restricted to pinhole cameras and unable to compute arbitrary light paths (e.g., reflections, refractions) [S5]. Linear scaling with Gaussian density for small Gaussians due to tile-based evaluation, unlike ray tracing which can scale logarithmically [S5].
 
 ## Background
-Originated with the "3D Gaussian Splatting" paper by Kerbl et al. (2023) [S1, unofficial][S5]. The method was developed to address the slow rendering speeds of previous 3D Gaussian representations by leveraging GPU tile-based rasterization techniques common in traditional graphics. It has since become the standard rendering backend for most 3DGS implementations, with numerous optimizations added for mobile, large-scale, and dynamic scenes [S1, S2, S3, unofficial].
+Originated with the 3D Gaussian Splatting paper by Kerbl et al. (2023) [S1, unofficial][S5]. It was developed to enable real-time, high-quality novel view synthesis from sparse images, leveraging the efficiency of tile-based rasterization adapted from traditional graphics pipelines [S1, unofficial][S5]. The method has become the standard for rendering 3D Gaussian Splatting scenes due to its balance of speed and quality [S5].
 
